"""Tirisfal: story speeches, and places named where the old accept line used to carry them."""

STORY = {
    'etc_ghouls': ("It's the same tunnels and a faster problem. The Scourge Ghouls came down with the Scourge, and they stayed when it left, because the cult keeps a plague cellar down here and leaves things out for them.\n\nSo they're fed, and a fed pack isn't the starving rabble you've met out on the glades. They're fast, they hunt properly, and they turn together.\n\nI've lost two of the Deathguard to them in the rib tunnels this month. Thin them out, and don't let them get behind you in the ribs.", 'Fight them with a wall at your back. They circle before they close.'),
    'etc_abomination': ("The skull, then, and what's wired into it.\n\nThe Abomination is at the head of the spine, and the cult has run their rune stones straight into it. That's what the whole farm has been doing up there, all those calling hours in the yard. I've watched them at it for a season, and they weren't summoning anything. They were feeding something that was already here.\n\nTake the necromancers off it first. They're the ones keeping the wards lit, and while the wards are lit you'll be hitting a thing that can't be allowed to fall over.\n\nThen put it down, and the cellar goes back to being a cellar, and this hill goes back to being a hill with an unfortunate history instead of one with a future.", 'Bring something to pry with. The stones are set deep into the bone.'),
    'wto_korgal': ("And there he is, in the centre of the camp under the standard. That's Meven Korgal.\n\nHe's a veteran. He was a veteran before he was a zealot, which is the dangerous order to do it in. He can hold a line, he can site a camp, and he prays like a man reading a map. Everything in that outpost that works, works because he's standing in it.\n\nHis Scarlet Converts will rally to him. They adore him, and you'll have to go through that first. But I've watched this camp for a season, and I'll tell you what I've concluded: take Korgal and the rest is tents.", None),
    'eth_converts': ("I'm Hallam, scouting. Get down a bit, that's it.\n\nThat's the Crusader's Outpost, and that lot by the fire are this season's Scarlet Converts. Listen to them for a minute.\n\nThey're loud, aren't they? Young, loud, and about as prepared for a fight as a sack of apples. Hold onto that impression for exactly as long as it takes to remember they came here on purpose, to a place everyone told them was full of us, because a man in a tabard told them it would mean something.\n\nA month of drill and they're Initiates. Then they're a column, and the column comes up our road. Do it now, while it's still the easier job.", None),
    'wta_keys': ("There's something under the mill at Agamand Mills, north of here, that I haven't put in a report yet.\n\nThe cellars are holding farmers, living ones, taken off the Solliden road and the western plots, shackled to the posts in the cellar and left. They haven't been eaten or turned. They've been left, the way you leave a thing you've been told to keep and haven't been told what for.\n\nThe servants carry the keys on them. They won't give them up and they won't use them. They just carry them, the way they carry everything else in that house, because it's theirs to carry.\n\nGet me the keys. Whatever else we are, we aren't going to be the people who knew about that cellar and did the paperwork instead.", None),
    'wtf_farmhands': ("Let me say this properly, because the Deathguard sent a lad last month who didn't let me finish.\n\nMy name is Marren. Solliden was my husband's farm, and the dead out in its fields are his hands. I've taken a room in Brill, because I couldn't sleep with them in the yard. They stayed on after he died, which was good of them. Then they stayed on after they died.\n\nThey still work it, at all hours, with rusted tools, no crop and no direction, up and down rows that haven't held anything in years. They aren't dangerous. They just aren't stopping, and I can't watch it from this window any longer.\n\nGo gently with them. They were good men, and they worked hard for a man who didn't always deserve it. Solliden Farmstead is west of here.", None),
    'wtf_threshers': ("The other thing in my fields isn't dead. That's what I can't get anyone in Brill to understand.\n\nThe grain threshers still run. Nobody feeds them, nobody winds them, nobody has touched one since my husband was upright, and they go up and down those rows at all hours taking in whatever's in front of them. The blight got into the works somewhere, and now they don't stop at the headland the way they're built to. They just turn and come back.\n\nThey've had the last of the harvest. There's nothing out there now but stubble and each other, and a machine that's run out of crop starts looking at the fence. Break them for me. I'd rather lose the iron than find out what they settle on next. Solliden Farmstead is west of here.", None),
    'wtf_goodman': ("She doesn't look up from the window.\n\nHe's out by the far hedge. You'll know him, because he's the only one out there carrying a pitchfork instead of a hoe, and he's the only one smiling.\n\nThat's my husband, Goodman Solliden. He worked that ground for thirty years, and he was hard on the hands and harder on himself, and whatever is walking it now has his face and his stride and his grin, and the grin is wrong. I can carry the grief. It's the grin I can't carry any more.\n\nSome of the hands keep to him. You'll have to take them off him first. Then let him lie down.\n\nI'll walk home and sit at my own window while you do it. I won't watch, but I'd like to be there. Solliden Farmstead is west of here.", None),
    'wta_thurman': ("The mill-house itself, then, and the miller in it.\n\nThurman Agamand was the miller before he was the lord, which the family never liked mentioned. The lordship came late and came by plague, when everybody above him in the line stopped being available. He's in the house now with what's left of them, and they're very much a family still. They eat together, they sit in the right chairs, they keep to the rooms they always kept to.\n\nI've stood at that door and listened to a dead man ask a dead woman to pass something. I'd like that house back, and I'd like to stop hearing it.\n\nThe family will come at you together. They always do. That's rather the point of them. Agamand Mills is north of here.", None),
    'wtc_wights': ("I'm Keeper Halvard. The Agamand crypt gate is mine, and I haven't been through it in a long time, and I'd like to stop having to say that sentence.\n\nThe crypt is Agamand ground, vaults and name-stones, the whole dynasty laid out in order down there. My post is to keep it. Then the wights woke up in it, and a man with one lantern and a ring of keys doesn't clear a vault full of wights. So my post became standing at a door being very reliable about it, until they sent me here to ask for help.\n\nThey hold the vaults now. They've been moving things. I can hear stone going over stone at night, and I've a horrible idea they're tidying.\n\nGo down and clear them out. I'll hold the gate. I'm very good at the gate. The Agamand Family Crypt is north of here.", None),
    'etm_melrache': ("There's a Scarlet captain called Melrache camped in my yard. He's got men digging, opening graves, going through them, and calling it a crusade because the word makes a shovel into a sacrament.\n\nI want to be clear about my objection. I don't mind that he's Scarlet. I mind that these are pilgrims. They walked all that way. They chose this ground. Whatever they thought they were doing with their lives, they finished it by coming to lie near a man they believed was good, and that officer is pulling them out of the earth in handfuls to see if they're carrying anything.\n\nThe restless will be up around him when you go in. Then have a word with the captain. Faol's Rest is north-east of here.", None),
    'etg_ice': ("There's something else about that water, and you can test this yourself while you're out there.\n\nIt's cold, the wrong kind of cold. It's colder than the air above it, in every season, and it has been since before I took the post. Lake water doesn't do that.\n\nThe shades along the shore hoard shards of black ice. They carry them about, they don't use them for anything, and wherever a shade has been standing a while the ground is hard. I've a theory that the shards are the cause rather than the symptom, and I'd like to be proved right or wrong before another winter.\n\nStrip the shards off them and bring them back to me. If I'm wrong I'll say so. Brightwater Lake is north of here.", None),
    'etg_bethor': ("Right, now the middle of the lake, and the reason none of the rest of it has worked.\n\nBethor Iceshard is a shade, or he was. He's got too cold to finish dying, and I can't explain that to you any better than that. He stands out there at the centre and pulls the warmth out of whatever comes near. Birds go down over that water. I've seen it.\n\nThe weeping ones keep close to him, and they'll come for you first. They always do. Take them off him, then put him out.\n\nAnd listen, don't stand still out there. Whatever he does, he does it to things that stop moving. Brightwater Lake is north of here.", None),
    'etr_mother': ("Now the one that matters. Every spider in that Vale came out of her.\n\nMother Venomweb is venom-fat, old, and clever in a way I've stopped being comfortable about. She doesn't rush, she doesn't panic, and twice now she's moved her brood before I got to where they were. She's doing arithmetic in there.\n\nHer skitterers will come at you in a wave when she feels you coming, so break those first, or you'll be stood still when she arrives, and standing still is the whole trick of that wood.\n\nI've been working up to this for a long time. I'd rather not do it alone. Venomweb Vale is east of here.", None),
    'etd_acolytes': ("I'm Warden Ysolde Grave. When you go up to the Cult's farm, keep the barn between you and the farmhouse.\n\nThe Cult of the Damned has the farm. They aren't raiders or a warband, they're a congregation, and they work it like a farm. That unsettles people who go up there expecting something theatrical.\n\nThe acolytes are the ones doing the calling. They stand in the yard at the hours they've been told to stand in it and reach for things that ought not to be reachable, and every time they do it the ground up there gets a little worse. The Undercity wants them silent. I want them silent for reasons that are mine.\n\nStart in the yard. They don't post a watch. They genuinely don't expect to be interrupted. The farm is east of here.", None),
    'ets_preachers': ("Now the ones at the back, and I want you taking these seriously, because I didn't, once.\n\nThe preachers don't carry much, and that's the mistake. You look at a man in robes behind two ranks of zealots and you make a judgement about which of them is the problem.\n\nThey call fire down. I don't mean a torch or a flask. I mean fire, out of nothing, onto whoever they've decided is faithless, and they decide fast. I've got the scars under this sleeve from the day I learned to pick them out of a line first.\n\nPick them out of the line first. That's the whole of the advice, and it's all I've got. The Scarlet Watch Post is north-east of here.", None),
    'ets_vishas': ("And the cells. I've left this one until last on purpose, and I won't dress it up.\n\nInterrogator Vishas runs the Watch Post's cells. He's patient, he's methodical, and he keeps proper hours. He stops for the evening, which is somehow the detail that's stayed with me.\n\nThere are people in there. Some of them are living, taken off the coast road. Some of them are ours, which the Crusade considers a different category of question and asks differently. Nobody has come out of those cells, in either direction, in the four months I've been on this slope.\n\nHis zealots hold the stair. Go through them, and empty the cells, and don't leave that man upright behind you. The Scarlet Watch Post is north-east of here.", None),
}
